BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ZAPlU0H1IX6IJVOw-3JJ3
SUMMARY:Intoxication in British Literature\, 1660-1760
DTSTAMP:20260805T053722Z
DTSTART:20260810T160000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Rowan Morar  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis Defen
	se  Department: English  "Intoxication in British Literature\, 1660-1760" 
	argues for reading literary representations of intoxicants and altered sta
	tes of mind\, as a normative condition of Enlightenment life and a feature
	 of literary representation itself. Drawing from social history\, I trace 
	how mass markets for drugs\, such as plantation produced tobacco and spiri
	ts\, as well as opium left their imprints on the works of key authors of B
	ritish literary history—Lord Rochester\, Aphra Behn\, Daniel Defoe\, and C
	harlotte Lennox. I argue that these writers from 1660-1760\, the Restorati
	on to the mid-Enlightenment period\, represented the influx of colonially 
	produced drugs that became fiscal necessities for statecraft in order to m
	ake tacit assumptions about everyday self-regulation\, socialization\, and
	 politics visible. Profane appetites were as fundamentally changed in Brit
	ain as they were central to concepts of subjectivity in the violent coloni
	al theaters producing the intoxicants for an era of taste and refinement. 
	The construction of a subjective experience of intoxication in poems\, the
	 performance of drunkenness on the stage\, the narrative accounts of inner
	 consciousness affected by rum or tobacco\, wine or opium—all offer means 
	for seeing double: the work of intoxicants on literature and literary into
	xication during an increasingly global era of mass intoxication.  &#160\; 
	 https://riceuniversity.zoom.us/s/92686072682  (Department : English)\n\nh
