UID:VffVXtLJ1oob8j8x7bYvP
SUMMARY:An Apparatus for Quantum Simulation with Rydberg Atoms
DTSTAMP:20260805T053722Z
DTSTART:20260810T180000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Brent Kruzel  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis Defe
	nse  Department: Physics &amp\; Astronomy  Location: Brockman Hall for Phy
	sics 300  This thesis presents the design\, construction\, and characteriz
	ation of a new experimental platform for quantum simulation using ultracol
	d strontium-88 Rydberg atoms. The apparatus was developed to support futur
	e optical-tweezer based experiments that realize synthetic dimensions thro
	ugh coherent millimeter-wave coupling between neighboring Rydberg states w
	hile providing the optical access\, electric- and magnetic-field control\,
	 and state-sensitive detection required for precision measurements. A new 
	science chamber was designed and integrated with laser cooling\, selective
	 field ionization\, and charged-particle detection systems\, while electro
	magnetic simulations were used to optimize millimeter-wave delivery and fi
	eld uniformity. Experimental characterization of the completed apparatus i
	ncludes measurements of static electric-field compensation\, millimeter-wa
	ve polarization purity\, and coherent Rydberg spectroscopy through Rabi an
	d Ramsey techniques. In addition\, analysis methods were developed to extr
	act relative Rydberg-state populations from selective field ionization sig
	nals\, and new high-precision microwave spectroscopy measurements were inc
	orporated into an updated determination of the quantum defect Rydberg-Ritz
	 parameters for the singlet S\, P\, and D Rydberg series of strontium. Tog
	ether\, these developments establish the experimental and analytical found
	ation for future quantum simulation experiments on this new apparatus.  &#
	160\;  (Department : Physics &amp\; Astronomy)\n\nhttps://events.rice.edu/
	event/435606-an-apparatus-for-quantum-simulation-with-rydberg
URL:https://events.rice.edu/event/435606-an-apparatus-for-quantum-simulatio
	n-with-rydberg
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Brockman Hall for Physics
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
