BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eqAE8AMH1ZvT5LRQ45GxW
SUMMARY:Physics-informed machine learning for reconstructing dark matter an
	d neutrino signals in TPCs
DTSTAMP:20260805T053722Z
DTSTART:20260813T150000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Ivy Li  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis Defense  D
	epartment: Physics &amp\; Astronomy  Location: Herman Brown Hall HBH 227  
	Dual-phase xenon time projection chamber (TPC) experiments such as XENONnT
	 are sensitive probes of dark matter and neutrinos\, but as these detector
	s grow more complex and data-intensive\, distinguishing signals of interes
	t from backgrounds depends on improved data handling and novel analysis te
	chniques. This thesis introduces multiple machine learning applications to
	ward position and energy reconstruction for these detectors. Initial studi
	es of semi-supervised autoencoders with partially interpretable latent spa
	ces and recurrent neural networks for correcting ionization signal wavefor
	ms demonstrated their limitations from the lack of physics constraints. Co
	ntinuous normalizing flow models were developed to learn the electric fiel
	d inside TPCs with physics-motivated constraints built into their architec
	ture. With a learned electric field\, the flow model is able to correct fo
	r field distortions in position reconstruction. Compared to traditional me
	thods\, the flow model reduces calibration data requirements by 50 times\,
	 its physics-informed architecture ensures that the learned electric field
	 is curl-free\, and the improvement in position reconstruction consequentl
	y improves charge yield estimation across the energy range of interest for
	 both WIMP and neutrinoless double beta decay searches. Finally\, the thes
	is presents a demonstration of the flow's applications towards a probabili
	stic fiducial volume in next-generation experiments.  &#160\;  Zoom: https
	://riceuniversity.zoom.us/j/93454420074?pwd=0ePCCADW4tBbylIlayywSkxCBMyTgp
	.1  (Department : Physics &amp\; Astronomy)\n\nhttps://events.rice.edu/eve
	nt/435990-physics-informed-machine-learning-for
URL:https://events.rice.edu/event/435990-physics-informed-machine-learning-
	for
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Herman Brown Hall
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ePz4zThBRcg17z2xeHBl4
SUMMARY:Illuminating the dynamics of magmatic systems: from bubbles to the 
	lithosphere\, from a computer to the lab
DTSTAMP:20260805T053722Z
DTSTART:20260813T190000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Gaetano Ferrante  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis 
	Defense  Department: Earth\, Environmental and Planetary Sciences  Locatio
	n: Keith-Wiess Geological Laboratories KWGL 123  Volcanoes are the surface
	 manifestation of Earth's great geodynamic forces. They provide an invalua
	ble window into geologic processes that are hidden from direct observation
	\, yet they represent a significant hazard for millions of people around t
	he world. Investigating the dynamics of volcanic systems is thus crucial t
	o better understand our planet and improve assessments of volcanic hazard.
	 Volcanic processes occur over a wide range of spatial and temporal scales
	\, motivating the use of correspondingly diverse methodologies to investig
	ate their dynamics. In this thesis\, I will employ experiments\, numerical
	 modeling and data analysis to address some of the most fundamental\, yet 
	widely disputed\, questions in volcanology.  One of the major controls on 
	the eruptive style and ensuing hazard of a volcano is the viscosity of its
	 magma. Among other factors\, gas bubbles suspended in the magma can eithe
	r increase or decrease its viscosity\, strongly influencing eruptive dynam
	ics. In Chapter 1\, I will present results from rheology experiments condu
	cted on natural pumice samples to understand how bubbles affect the viscos
	ity of magma. I will propose a new and unambiguous constitutive equation f
	or the viscosity of bubbly magmas that can be used to better model volcani
	c eruptions and lava flows.  Magmatic systems can extend for tens of kilom
	eters from the asthenosphere\, where magma is produced\, to the Earth's su
	rface\, where it is eventually erupted. Whether volcanoes are controlled f
	rom the bottom-up or from the top-down is still a major question in volcan
	ology. In Chapter 2\, I will couple a numerical model of magma flow at the
	 lithospheric scale with surface-available observations collected during t
	he past 30 years at Kīlauea volcano (Hawaiʻi)\, to shed light on the dynam
	ics of its magmatic supply pathway.  "Why do eruptions end?" is a fundamen
	tal\, yet overlooked question in volcanology. In 2018\, Kīlauea experience
	d its largest eruption in more than 200 years of history\, providing an un
	precedented set of observations that helped volcanologists improve our und
	erstanding of caldera-forming eruptions. More than 8 years after the start
	 of the eruption\, the reasons underlying its sudden and unexpected conclu
	sion still remain\, for the most part\, a mystery. In Chapter 3\, I will j
	ointly analyze a diverse set of geophysical data collected during Kīlauea'
	s 2018 eruption to shed light on its progression and enigmatic end\, provi
	ding a framework to interpret the evolution of past and future caldera-for
	ming eruptions.  &#160\;  https://riceuniversity.zoom.us/j/4149242694?pwd=
	YWZsUDhEVWthT3E3R041SkpNMk5uQT09&amp\;omn=99605727293  (Department : Earth
	\, Environmental and Planetary Sciences)\n\nhttps://events.rice.edu/event/
	435787-illuminating-the-dynamics-of-magmatic-systems-from
URL:https://events.rice.edu/event/435787-illuminating-the-dynamics-of-magma
	tic-systems-from
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Keith-Wiess Geological Laboratories
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:rdN78rj0WIhoVDulkyiPl
SUMMARY:Bayesian Inference and Medical AI: From Period Estimation to Cardia
	c Volume Prediction
DTSTAMP:20260805T053722Z
DTSTART:20260813T200000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Tony Gao  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis Defense 
	 Department: Statistics  This thesis presents three projects unified by th
	eir focus on developing and applying statistical and machine learning meth
	ods for robust parameter estimation across different scientific domains. I
	n the first project\, the accurate estimation of the periodicity of variab
	le stars is important for understanding their physical properties and beha
	viors. While current methods may give accurate point estimates\, uncertain
	ty quantification is often missing. We develop a fast semi-parametric Baye
	sian model for period estimation that provides principled uncertainty quan
	tification. We establish asymptotic consistency of the period estimator an
	d apply the method to variable stars from the OGLE II project. The subsequ
	ent two projects focus on automating diagnosis in medical imaging with Art
	ificial Intelligence (AI) models. Emerging AI models provide accurate left
	 ventricular volume estimates from echocardiograms (echo) compared to huma
	n experts when echo is used as the reference. However\, a combination of p
	oor image quality\, human error\, and volume assumptions of echo may not p
	rovide accurate estimation. We therefore use cardiac magnetic resonance im
	aging (CMR) estimates as the reference standard because they are more accu
	rate and reproducible. We apply and assess two state-of-the-art foundation
	al models in a novel Houston Methodist cohort in which patients underwent 
	both echo and CMR within 24 hours of each other. Finally\, we develop a cu
	stom AI model fine-tuned on these data\, which substantially improves upon
	 the off-the-shelf models and performs comparably to human experts.  https
	://riceuniversity.zoom.us/j/93352464646?pwd=qDte6U4fVL4UU90ACeCbLyhiz8vxgj
	.1  &#160\;  (Department : Statistics)\n\nhttps://events.rice.edu/event/43
	5969-bayesian-inference-and-medical-ai-from-period
URL:https://events.rice.edu/event/435969-bayesian-inference-and-medical-ai-
	from-period
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
