UID:67Poifo9-kXW-r5gWNlJN
SUMMARY:Binder Jetting Process Optimization: Predicting Powder Spreading Vi
	a Rheology and Enhancing Jetting Performance through Machine Learning
DTSTAMP:20260805T053722Z
DTSTART:20260814T140000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Wesley Combs  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis Defe
	nse  Department: Mechanical Engineering  Location: Mechanical Engineering 
	Building 128  Binder jet 3D printing (BJ3DP) is a prominent powder-based a
	dditive manufacturing (PBAM) technique responsible for the production of h
	igh-performance parts in multiple industries including the aerospace\, aut
	omotive\, medical\, tooling\, and weapons fields. Low-density green parts 
	are constructed by an iterative approach involving the spreading of powder
	 into smooth layers and the subsequent deposition of binder droplets to fo
	rm solid cross-sectional regions. A high-temperature densification process
	 known as sintering is typically conducted to yield high-density end-use p
	arts. Prior to sintering\, powder flow in both the dry spreading and wet j
	etting systems is highly influential to green part dimensional accuracy\, 
	surface roughness\, and mechanical properties. Furthermore\, high-quality 
	green parts tend to produce good end-use parts. The efficiency of BJ3DP ca
	n be increased through a variety of means\, including qualification testin
	g and analysis software. Powder flowability is often tested prior to print
	ing to inform material selection and powder design\, especially when deali
	ng with previously unqualified materials\, particle size distributions (PS
	Ds)\, and powder treatment methods. Additionally\, many machine learning (
	ML) tools have been developed for real-time powder bed inspection as well 
	as part quality prediction. However\, the majority of existing analysis in
	 these two realms is specifically tailored to powder bed fusion\, the othe
	r major PBAM technique.  &#160\;  A collection of BJ3DP powder flow analys
	es and jetting phase ML models are presented herein with various direct ap
	plications. First\, an extensive study of powder rheology and additive man
	ufacturing suitability is conducted on extraterrestrial regolith simulants
	. Space travel and colonization efforts are supported by mass hopper flow 
	design according to Jenike Stress Theory and landing site suggestions info
	rmed by in situ resource utilization PBAM. Three Ti64 alloy powders common
	ly used in BJ3DP received additive manufacturing suitability factors (AMS)
	 only 26-46% better than coarse lunar highland simulant (LHS-1) and only 3
	3-51% better than Martian simulant with high sulfate concentration (MGS-1S
	). This increases confidence in using actual regolith as AM feedstock mate
	rial and helps inform which types of lunar and Martian soil might perform 
	best. Next\, a similar experimental study is completed with a fully-recycl
	ed nickel superalloy called M247 and three conventionally-sourced steel po
	wders (2205\, 316\, and 17-4). The feasibility of powder reclamation is su
	pported by the relatively exceptional flow characteristics of M247\, which
	 outperforms the steel powders in terms of AMS by around 20%. A review of 
	correlations between powder properties and flow parameters in metal powder
	s shows that this was largely due to the high density and adequate particl
	e size of M247. This demonstrates that the significant reduction in materi
	al cost provided by re-atomization does not necessitate a loss in powder s
	preadability.  Moving on to the ML models\, a set of multilayer perceptron
	s are trained to use PSD parameters to predict jetting-induced particle mo
	tion and saturation. High accuracy was exhibited by the model as it receiv
	ed low testing dataset mean absolute percentage errors (MAPE) between 5.9%
	 and 16.7% for the four predicted macro-parameters. These values are consi
	derably lower than the threshold value of 25%\, which was based on the var
	iation of true output data between powder beds with nearly identical PSDs.
	 Models are designed to reduce heuristic printing and are trained on simul
	ations executed with an in-house computational fluid dynamics and discrete
	 element method framework. The models can be used to determine an importan
	t user-defined jetting parameter for binder jetting printers. They can als
	o be used to identify an optimal PSD that minimizes green part porosity an
	d dimensional error caused by particle displacement and terminal speed. Th
	e remaining ML model developed in this thesis is a physics-informed long s
	hort-term memory (LSTM) neural network that forecasts BJ3DP ejection volum
	e\, speed\, and height. The LSTM model conducts direct multi-step forecast
	ing over a 1 millisecond horizon based on printing process parameters and 
	recent particle ejection history. The model's predictions yielded a testin
	g dataset $R^2 &gt\; 0.9$ and low MAPE values of 24%\, 14.8%\, and 2.08% f
	or average ejection volume\, speed\, and height. These error values were s
	ignificantly less than the following MAPE values found for 63 pairs of tim
	e series with identical printing process parameters: 99% (volume)\, 58% (s
	peed)\, and 15% (height). In the future\, the model could be deployed to a
	ssist real-time monitoring by providing rapid printing process parameter m
	odification to improve part quality. The model could also identify anomalo
	us printing more quickly than manual human intervention\, reducing unneces
	sary printing waste. Collectively\, powder rheology and machine learning a
	re leveraged to provide useful information and helpful analysis tools to i
	mprove BJ3DP within ISRU efforts\, powder reclamation cycles\, and day-to-
	day industrial printing.  https://riceuniversity.zoom.us/j/95736070182?pwd
	=d3HLaWwkyjyJgxIPlb707zKek120rb.1  (Department : Mechanical Engineering)\n
	\nhttps://events.rice.edu/event/436128-binder-jetting-process-optimization
	-predicting
URL:https://events.rice.edu/event/436128-binder-jetting-process-optimizatio
	n-predicting
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Mechanical Engineering Building
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
