UID:7XObppuZO1kO7kuz22ijb
SUMMARY:The role of working memory in predictive processing during sentence
	 comprehension
DTSTAMP:20260805T053722Z
DTSTART:20260821T180000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Yu Lu  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis Defense  De
	partment: Psychology  Location: BioScience Research Collaborative 706  Lan
	guage comprehension is supported not only by integrating incoming words wi
	th prior information but also by using the preceding context to predict up
	coming information. Prediction is an important process that contributes to
	 processing efficiency. However\, less is known about how predictive proce
	ssing relates to general cognitive resources. I addressed this issue by ex
	amining the role of verbal working memory (WM) in prediction during senten
	ce comprehension using several methodological approaches\, examining eye-m
	ovements\, behavioral measures\, and EEG components. Experiment 1 used the
	 visual world paradigm to examine the relation of individual differences i
	n semantic WM\, phonological WM\, visual-spatial WM\, processing speed\, a
	nd other verbal factors to verb-mediated anticipatory eye movements. Criti
	cally\, semantic WM but none of the other individual differences measures 
	independently predicted the anticipatory eye movement effect. Experiment 2
	 used a sentence-completion task to examine the role of WM in sentence-fin
	al word predictions. Sentence WM load was manipulated by including conditi
	ons with prediction-irrelevant clauses placed either before or between pre
	diction-relevant information. Global prediction accuracy declined as infor
	mation load increased and further decreased when irrelevant information wa
	s at an intervening position. Participants also became more likely to gene
	rate locally supported predictions based on adjacent context. When examini
	ng individual differences factors\, semantic WM\, but not phonological WM\
	, was a significant predictor. Experiment 3 tested whether the global-to-l
	ocal shift in predictions would also appear in a comprehension task withou
	t overt production demand. Participants read short and long sentence conte
	xts followed by globally expected\, locally expected\, or implausible endi
	ngs while EEG was recorded. Longer contexts reduced acceptance of globally
	 expected endings and increased acceptance of locally expected endings. Th
	e N400 was graded by ending type but was not modulated by sentence length.
	 In contrast\, the late frontal positivity showed a Length x Ending intera
	ction\, suggesting that information load affected later prediction revisio
	n. Together\, the findings suggested that prediction involves both a more 
	passive and an active component\, that automatic semantic activation opera
	tes alongside resource-dependent mechanisms that require maintaining and c
	ombining prior context. Semantic WM appears especially important in the ac
	tive prediction process\, supporting the construction and maintenance of t
	he meaning representation from which predictions are generated. When earli
	er context becomes less accessible\, prediction and interpretation shift t
	oward emphasizing recent local information.  &#160\;  (Department : Psycho
	logy)\n\nhttps://events.rice.edu/event/436045-the-role-of-working-memory-i
	n-predictive
URL:https://events.rice.edu/event/436045-the-role-of-working-memory-in-pred
	ictive
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:BioScience Research Collaborative
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:O-uuWtXswk8hkSAqeoGJM
SUMMARY:Current Research in EEPS: Dr. Bridger Ruyle\, NYU Tandon School of 
	Engineering
DTSTAMP:20260805T053722Z
DTSTART:20260827T180000Z
DESCRIPTION:Per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances (PFAS) are anthropogenic co
	mpounds that contain at least one –CF3 or –CF2– group and are known as for
	ever chemicals because they cannot fully mineralize in the environment. Al
	though essential for modern agriculture\, medicine\, refrigeration\, and e
	lectronics\, among other industries\, PFAS contamination poses risks to hu
	man health and contributes to climate change. Most research and public att
	ention focuses on a few PFAS. This narrow focus overlooks the many hundred
	s/thousands of additional PFAS known as precursors that can transform into
	 stable\, terminal products in the environment. This presentation will hig
	hlight recent field work\, experimental results\, data synthesis\, and mod
	eling efforts aimed at improving precursor quantification using organofluo
	rine mass balances and environmental fate\, including the importance of so
	il retention and transformation rates. These studies will be used to discu
	ss the role of precursors on the severity and timescales of PFAS contamina
	tion at major point sources to the environment including firefighting trai
	ning facilities\, wastewater treatment facilities\, and farmlands.\n\nhttp
	s://events.rice.edu/event/436101-current-research-in-eeps-dr-bridger-ruyle
	-nyu
URL:https://events.rice.edu/event/436101-current-research-in-eeps-dr-bridge
	r-ruyle-nyu
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Keith-Wiess Geological Laboratories
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
